# Steady-State Temperature Analysis v0.1

## Status

Completed.

A preliminary 1D steady-state thermal analysis was performed
for the GNSS L1 Helix antenna using MATLAB.

## Simulation

- Simulation duration: 24 hours
- Initial temperature: 20 °C
- Number of thermal nodes: 50
- Eclipse: OFF

## Thermal Model

The model includes:

- Solar heating
- Albedo heating
- Earth infrared heating
- Axial thermal conduction along the Helix
- Thermal radiation to space

The current model does not yet include:

- Ground Plane thermal coupling
- Feed thermal conduction
- Detailed radiation view factors
- Temperature-dependent material properties
- Validated optical surface properties
- Detailed thermal contact resistance

## Steady-State Results

| Parameter | Result |
|---|---:|
| Simulation time | 24 h |
| Minimum temperature | 20.0000 °C |
| Maximum temperature | 21.0651 °C |
| Mean temperature | 20.6975 °C |
| Temperature range | 1.0651 °C |
| Last-step temperature change | 0 K |

## Interpretation

The model reached a numerically stable temperature
distribution at the end of the 24-hour simulation.

The maximum temperature was 21.0651 °C,
while the minimum temperature was 20.0000 °C.

This is a preliminary steady-state result and should not
yet be considered the final thermal prediction of the
complete antenna system.

## Output

The following files were generated:

- `steady_state_temperature_v01.png`
- `steady_state_temperature_v01.csv`
- `steady_state_temperature_v01.mat`

## Next Step

The next stage is to introduce the Ground Plane thermal coupling
and develop the coupled thermal model.
